---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro';
import Claim from '../../components/Claim.astro';
import Answer from '../../components/Answer.astro';
import Disclosure from '../../components/Disclosure.astro';
import FigureFamilies from '../../components/figures/FigureFamilies.astro';
import FigureSurfaces from '../../components/figures/FigureSurfaces.astro';

// The two diagrams on this page are the FigureFamilies and FigureSurfaces
// components, rendered in the template below.

const body = `<div class="callout callout--blue">
<span class="callout__title">Two RNG call surfaces</span>
The source contains call sites using <code>ngu.random.bytes()</code> and others using <code>ckcc.rng_bytes()</code>. The affected symbol-resolution path sits behind the first surface. The second reaches the STM32 hardware RNG directly. This distinction identifies which features require further analysis; it does not by itself establish the full security consequence for every feature.
</div>`;

// The right-hand column records whether Wizardsardine's post-mortem asserted
// the exposure. It replaces a separate adjudication table that restated ten of
// these rows, quoting the same source comments.
const rest = `<div class="table-wrap">
<div class="table-scroll">
<table class="data-table sortable">
<caption>Randomness call-site classification</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope="col" class="wide">Secret or material</th><th scope="col" class="nw">Source</th><th scope="col" class="nw">RNG path before the fix</th><th scope="col" class="wide">Scope established here</th><th scope="col" class="nw">Wizardsardine list</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="row-gone"><td class="wide"><strong>Master wallet seed</strong>, and ephemeral or temporary seeds</td><td class="nw"><code>seed.py:605</code></td><td class="nw"><span class="status status--bad">Affected API</span></td><td class="wide">Confirmed use of the affected generation path. Ephemeral and temporary seeds call the same generation function; their downstream use must be assessed by feature.</td><td class="nw">&mdash;</td></tr>
<tr><td class="wide">Paper-wallet private keys</td><td class="nw"><code>paper.py:94</code></td><td class="nw"><span class="status status--bad">Affected API</span></td><td class="wide">The device-generated path uses the affected API: <code>paper.py:94</code> constructs <code>ngu.secp256k1.keypair()</code> with no argument, and the libngu constructor at <code>k1.c:485-487</code> fills the secret key from <code>my_random_bytes()</code> in exactly that case. The separate pure-dice path at <code>paper.py:191-202</code> passes its own private key in, so it never reaches that branch.</td><td class="nw">Asserted</td></tr>
<tr><td class="wide">Seed XOR split masks</td><td class="nw"><code>xor_seed.py:78</code></td><td class="nw"><span class="status status--bad">Affected API</span></td><td class="wide">Mask generation uses the affected API, and only in the optional random-split mode selected at <code>xor_seed.py:49-52</code>. The prompt string at <code>xor_seed.py:49</code> reads "If you would prefer a random split using the TRNG, press (2)", and the branch it selects at <code>xor_seed.py:77-79</code> is the one calling <code>ngu.random.bytes</code>. The default deterministic mode does not. The information leaked by a particular share set requires separate analysis.</td><td class="nw">Asserted</td></tr>
<tr><td class="wide">Key Teleport ECDH material and password</td><td class="nw"><code>teleport.py:79,204,229</code></td><td class="nw"><span class="status status--bad">Affected API</span></td><td class="wide">Random ECDH-related material reaches this path. The receiver password is five bytes, and the source comment at <code>teleport.py:226-229</code> reads "pick an 40 bit password" above <code>ngu.random.bytes(5)</code>. Protocol impact has not been independently demonstrated here.</td><td class="nw">Asserted</td></tr>
<tr><td class="wide">Key Teleport co-signer derivation index</td><td class="nw"><code>multisig.py:1190</code></td><td class="nw"><span class="status status--bad">Affected API</span></td><td class="wide">The index used to derive a co-signer's receive key is drawn as <code>ngu.random.uniform(1&lt;&lt;28)</code>, so its intended width is 28 bits before any generator weakness is considered. Whether either Key Teleport width is reachable in a live protocol run is not established here.</td><td class="nw">Asserted</td></tr>
<tr><td class="wide">Clone and USB session material</td><td class="nw"><code>backups.py:752,862</code><br><code>usb.py:715</code></td><td class="nw"><span class="status status--bad">Affected API</span></td><td class="wide">Session-related values reach this path. <code>backups.py:752</code> and <code>backups.py:862</code> both create argument-free keypairs, and the second derives the archive encryption key by <code>ecdh_multiply</code>. Exploitability depends on transcript access and protocol use; the post-mortem itself describes the USB case as not significant.</td><td class="nw">Asserted</td></tr>
<tr><td class="wide">Web2FA secret and nonces</td><td class="nw"><code>web2fa.py:25,110,145,148</code></td><td class="nw"><span class="status status--bad">Affected API</span></td><td class="wide">Secret or nonce generation reaches this path. The account-level consequence depends on what an attacker can observe.</td><td class="nw">Asserted</td></tr>
<tr><td class="wide">HSM local confirmation code</td><td class="nw"><code>hsm.py:857</code></td><td class="nw"><span class="status status--bad">Affected API</span></td><td class="wide">The confirmation-code call site uses the affected API.</td><td class="nw">Asserted</td></tr>
<tr><td class="wide">HSM policy-approval character</td><td class="nw"><code>hsm_ux.py:58</code></td><td class="nw"><span class="status status--bad">Affected API</span></td><td class="wide">The character a user must press to enable a new HSM policy is chosen from a five-item set through the affected API. This is a presence prompt rather than key material.</td><td class="nw">Asserted</td></tr>
<tr><td class="wide">MicroSD 2FA enrolment nonce</td><td class="nw"><code>pwsave.py:318</code></td><td class="nw"><span class="status status--bad">Affected API</span></td><td class="wide">The eight-byte nonce written when enrolling an authorised card uses the affected API. What that means at the account level remains untested in this archive.</td><td class="nw">Omitted</td></tr>
<tr><td class="wide">Secure Notes and Passwords random generators</td><td class="nw"><code>notes.py:58-92</code></td><td class="nw"><span class="status status--bad">Affected API</span></td><td class="wide">The 12-word, 24-word and dense-password choices at <code>notes.py:66-78</code> call <code>generate_seed()</code>. The mixed-format choice uses <code>ngu.random</code> through <code>random.randbelow()</code> and <code>shuffle()</code>, and its own source comment at <code>notes.py:83</code> reads <code>entropy: 11+11 + (3.8*3) + 16 = 49 bits</code>. That budget is the design intent before this defect, not a measurement of delivered strength. The separate BIP85 choice is classified below.</td><td class="nw">Asserted</td></tr>
<tr><td class="wide">Co-signing policy key C</td><td class="nw"><code>ccc.py:899</code></td><td class="nw"><span class="status status--bad">Affected API</span></td><td class="wide">The 12 words the device retains to enforce a spending policy are produced by the same <code>generate_seed()</code> function as a wallet seed. The import paths at <code>ccc.py:870-895</code> supply words from elsewhere instead.</td><td class="nw">Asserted</td></tr>
<tr><td class="wide">Randomised PIN keypad layout</td><td class="nw"><code>login.py:32-34</code></td><td class="nw"><span class="status status--warn">Affected API</span></td><td class="wide">The shuffle uses <code>ngu.random</code> through <code>shared/random.py</code>, and therefore <code>ngu.random.uniform</code>. This is shoulder-surfing and wear-pattern hardening, not key material, and any consequence requires physical observation.</td><td class="nw">Asserted</td></tr>
<tr><td class="wide"><strong>BIP85-derived entropy, mnemonics and passwords</strong></td><td class="nw"><code>drv_entro.py:57-112</code></td><td class="nw"><span class="status status--warn">Deterministic derivation</span></td><td class="wide">BIP85 derives child entropy from the active BIP32 root rather than calling the affected RNG again. If that root is recoverable, the same application and index paths can be rederived. An active BIP39 passphrase remains part of the root from which the child is derived.</td><td class="nw">Asserted</td></tr>
<tr><td class="wide">Trick-PIN duress wallets</td><td class="nw"><code>trick_pins.py:14,80</code></td><td class="nw"><span class="status status--warn">Deterministic derivation</span></td><td class="wide">Duress wallet secrets come from <code>bip85_derive</code>, and the device's own prompt at <code>trick_pins.py:597</code> describes indices 1 to 3. They follow the root rather than adding independent randomness.</td><td class="nw">Asserted</td></tr>
<tr><td class="wide">MicroSD 2FA file encryption key</td><td class="nw"><code>pwsave.py:39-47</code></td><td class="nw"><span class="status status--warn">Deterministic derivation</span></td><td class="wide">The key is derived from the wallet secret and a card-identity salt, so it follows the seed's provenance. This is separate from the enrolment nonce listed above.</td><td class="nw">Asserted</td></tr>
<tr><td class="wide">MuSig2 session randomness</td><td class="nw"><code>libngu k1.c:836</code></td><td class="nw"><span class="status status--bad">Affected API</span></td><td class="wide">The session-randomness call site uses the affected API. Nonce reuse or predictability can be security-critical, but no incident-specific exploit is established here.</td><td class="nw">Omitted</td></tr>
<tr><td class="wide">secp256k1 context blinding</td><td class="nw"><code>libngu k1.c:110-112</code></td><td class="nw"><span class="status status--warn">Affected API</span></td><td class="wide"><code>ctx_randomize()</code> fills its 32 blinding bytes from <code>my_random_bytes()</code>. This is side-channel hardening rather than wallet-key generation. Residual protection is not assessed here.</td><td class="nw">Asserted</td></tr>
<tr class="row-safe"><td class="wide"><strong>Backup file password, 12 words</strong></td><td class="nw"><code>backups.py:337</code></td><td class="nw"><span class="status status--ok">Intact path</span></td><td class="wide">Uses <code>ckcc.rng_bytes()</code>, the hardware TRNG. The password path is separate from the provenance of the mnemonic inside the backup.</td><td class="nw">&mdash;</td></tr>
<tr class="row-safe"><td class="wide">7z archive salt and IV</td><td class="nw"><code>compat7z.py:23</code></td><td class="nw"><span class="status status--ok">Intact</span></td><td class="wide">Hardware TRNG.</td><td class="nw">&mdash;</td></tr>
<tr class="row-safe"><td class="wide">HSM per-user secrets</td><td class="nw"><code>users.py:182</code></td><td class="nw"><span class="status status--ok">Intact</span></td><td class="wide">Hardware TRNG, ten bytes. This is the one HSM secret on the intact path, which is why the HSM rows above are split rather than treated as one finding.</td><td class="nw">Narrowed</td></tr>
<tr class="row-safe"><td class="wide">SD card overwrite blocks</td><td class="nw"><code>files.py:65,124</code></td><td class="nw"><span class="status status--ok">Intact</span></td><td class="wide">Hardware TRNG.</td><td class="nw">&mdash;</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<p class="cap">Last column: whether the exposure appears in Wizardsardine's "broken regardless of your seed" enumeration. <em>Omitted</em> marks a call site on the affected surface that the enumeration does not list; <em>narrowed</em> marks one where this archive qualifies the assertion; an em dash marks material that enumeration does not cover either way.</p>

<div class="callout callout--amber">
<span class="callout__title">What this establishes for an encrypted backup</span>
Restoring the backup restores the same mnemonic. If that mnemonic used affected firmware, installing the hotfix or retaining a correctly generated backup password does not change its generation history. Retained copies therefore preserve the same generation history as the live wallet.
</div>`;
---

<Article
  title="Blast radius"
  description="The affected and unaffected randomness call surfaces identified in the firmware source, and the device features that call the affected generator without consulting your seed."
  standfirst="A sound mnemonic does not make the device sound. Beyond seed generation, a set of COLDCARD features drew their own secrets from the affected generator, so dice-only and imported seeds are outside only the first of the two failure families below."
  depth="deep"
  kicker="How it broke"
  next={{
    href: '/how-it-broke/',
    label: 'Generator path',
    note: 'Return to the reconstructed generation path and firmware mechanism.',
  }}
>
  <Answer>
    <p>
      How a mnemonic was produced decides whether the wallet keys themselves
      are enumerable, but that is not the whole
      defect. A second family of COLDCARD features asks the same generator for
      its own bytes and never consults the mnemonic at all, so the answer to
      "where did my seed come from" does not reach them. The register below
      classifies every randomness call site found in the firmware and libngu by
      which of the two generators it used.<Claim
        basis="verified"
        scope="The call-site classification between the affected ngu.random surface and the intact ckcc.rng_bytes surface"
        source="Call-site review of COLDCARD firmware and libngu at the cited paths"
        href="/how-it-broke/"
        compact
      />
    </p>
  </Answer>

  <h2>Two failure families, and only one of them is about your seed</h2>

  <FigureFamilies />

  <div class="ve-card ve-card--amber">
    <div class="ve-card__label lbl-amber">Who this second family reaches</div>
    <p>
      Anyone who used the listed features on affected firmware, including people
      who rolled 50 or more fair dice, people who imported a mnemonic generated
      on another device, and people whose wallet keys were never at issue. The
      exposures differ sharply in consequence: a paper wallet's private key is
      the generator's output outright, while a randomised PIN keypad only
      matters to someone watching the screen. The register grades each one
      separately rather than presenting the list as uniform risk.<Claim
        basis="unverified"
        scope="Feature-level exploitability beyond master-seed and device-generated paper-wallet key creation"
        source="The affected calls are verified, but no incident-specific exploit had been established here for each downstream feature as of a 6 August 2026 recheck"
        compact
      />
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="ported" set:html={body} />

  <FigureSurfaces />

  <div set:html={rest} />

  <h2 id="wizardsardine">Adjudicating the Wizardsardine feature list</h2>

  <p>
    On 1 August 2026 Wizardsardine published a
    <a href="/record/sources/wizardsardine-postmortem/">long-form post-mortem</a>
    whose "broken regardless of your seed" section is the most complete public
    enumeration of this second family captured here. Its author, Kevin Loaec,
    separately stressed on X that
    <a href="/record/sources/kloaec-derived-feature-exposure/">imported and dice-generated seeds are still exposed through those features</a>.
    The last column of the register above records what each call site carries
    from that enumeration: the mechanism it describes holds at every call site
    checked here, and several of its specific numbers are quotable from the
    source comments. This archive discloses the company's competing interest for
    the same reason it discloses
    <a href="/record/firmware/">Foundation Devices' competing interest</a> around
    the OPENDIME test: a vendor's analysis of a rival's product is evidence of
    what the vendor asserts, and is checked before it is repeated. Three things
    follow that the enumeration itself does not say.
  </p>

  <div class="ve-card ve-card--red">
    <div class="ve-card__label lbl-red">One claim needs narrowing: HSM secrets are split across both generators</div>
    <p>
      The Web2FA shared secret (<code>web2fa.py:110</code>), the local
      confirmation code (<code>hsm.py:857</code>) and the policy-approval
      character (<code>hsm_ux.py:58</code>) all use the affected API. But HSM
      per-user secrets at <code>users.py:182</code> use
      <code>ckcc.rng_bytes()</code>, the intact hardware path. "HSM secrets came
      from the same generator" is therefore true of the second factor and the
      confirmation prompts, and not true of the per-user credentials.<Claim
        basis="verified"
        scope="Every call site, source comment and prompt string quoted in the register above, and the narrowing of the HSM claim"
        source="COLDCARD firmware at commit 9a88e1a5 and the libngu clone held here, read at the cited paths"
        href="https://github.com/Coldcard/firmware/blob/9a88e1a5c32c69ba7ace7f22c26da90f8442b745/shared/paper.py"
      />
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    <strong>The enumeration is not exhaustive.</strong> It omits the MuSig2
    session-randomness call site at libngu <code>k1.c:836</code> and the microSD
    2FA enrolment nonce at <code>pwsave.py:318</code>, both of which this archive
    classifies on the affected surface. It should be read as substantially
    correct rather than complete.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Two onward conclusions are reported rather than verified here.</strong>
    That possession of a clone file is sufficient to reconstruct the session key
    and recover the mnemonic in clear is a protocol consequence this archive has
    not demonstrated, although the underlying call sites are confirmed. And that
    the predictable side-channel countermeasure is "therefore useless" is a
    conclusion about residual protection this archive has not assessed; the same
    call site is graded as hardening rather than key material in the register
    above.<Claim
      basis="reported"
      scope="Wizardsardine's onward security conclusions about clone-file decryption and residual side-channel protection, and its overall framing of the exposure"
      source="Wizardsardine post-mortem by Kevin Loaec, 1 Aug 2026. Competing interest: Wizardsardine sells Liana, a wallet product competing with COLDCARD's software surface; the post-mortem's first section is directed at Liana users and closes by recommending the company's institutional offering. The author also describes the post as written quickly under stress by three people in parallel, and invites corrections."
      href="/record/sources/wizardsardine-postmortem/"
    /> Whether any of these feature exposures has actually been exploited
    against a real device in this incident is unknown: no captured source
    demonstrates it, and the incident evidence held here concerns wallet
    keys.<Claim
      basis="unverified"
      scope="Whether any listed feature exposure has been exploited against a real device in this incident"
      source="No captured source demonstrates exploitation of a non-seed feature path as of a 6 August 2026 recheck; the incident evidence held here concerns wallet keys"
    />
  </p>

  <h2 id="passphrases">What a passphrase does against a recoverable seed</h2>

  <p>
    A BIP39 passphrase is a separate input to key derivation, so it survives
    the seed's generation defect: an attacker who recovers the mnemonic still
    has to find the passphrase. Two captured posts bear on how much that is
    worth in practice.
    <a href="/record/sources/jamesob-passphrase-verification/">James O'Beirne reported on 1 August</a>
    an LLM-assisted review across the firmware repository's release tags
    concluding that the BIP-39 passphrase integration itself is sound, while
    cautioning that a passphrase must be long and unwieldy before it is
    cryptographically relevant.<Claim
      basis="reported"
      scope="The LLM-assisted conclusion that the BIP-39 passphrase integration is sound across the checked firmware tags, and the caution that only a long, unwieldy passphrase is cryptographically relevant"
      source="James O'Beirne, captured X post, 1 Aug 2026; the review itself has not been reproduced by this archive"
      href="/record/sources/jamesob-passphrase-verification/"
    /> And on 2 August,
    <a href="/record/sources/btcsessions-passphrase-loss-report/">BTC Sessions reported</a>
    what he described as the first confirmed loss from a Mk3 wallet protected
    by a passphrase of two words, drained at around 2pm on 2 August,
    Australia time. That post cites no transaction or address, and no
    corroborating first-hand account is held here, so it stands as a single
    uncorroborated report.<Claim
      basis="reported"
      scope="The reported drain of a Mk3 wallet protected by a two-word passphrase at around 2pm on 2 Aug 2026, AEST"
      source="BTC Sessions, captured X post, 2 Aug 2026; no transaction or address is cited and no corroborating first-hand account is held here"
      href="/record/sources/btcsessions-passphrase-loss-report/"
    /> The two posts describe the same arithmetic from
    opposite ends: once an affected seed is recovered, a short passphrase is a
    small offline search on top of it. The
    <a href="/how-it-broke/conditions/#passphrase">passphrase record</a>
    preserves the source assumptions and the attributed guidance.
  </p>

  <h2 id="privacy">A reported privacy dimension</h2>

  <p>
    The register above concerns secrets an attacker could reproduce. Money is
    not the only thing a reproduced seed reaches:
    <a href="/record/sources/rawavocado-privacy-impact/">raw_avocado argued on 2 August</a>
    that the recoverable seed population is also a privacy failure, because
    anyone who derives a compromised seed obtains that wallet's complete
    transaction history along with its keys, enabling UTXO linkage and
    deanonymisation even for owners who have already moved their funds. On
    that argument, migration protects the balance but not the history the old
    wallet has already written to the chain. It is one author's argument,
    reported here without independent assessment.<Claim
      basis="reported"
      scope="The argument that deriving a compromised seed exposes the wallet's full history and enables UTXO linkage and deanonymisation, including for users who already migrated"
      source="raw_avocado, captured X post, 2 Aug 2026; one author's argument, not independently assessed here"
      href="/record/sources/rawavocado-privacy-impact/"
    />
  </p>

  <Disclosure
    id="bip85"
    title="BIP85 descendants follow the active root"
    note="why a child mnemonic's different words are not independent provenance"
  >
    <p>
      <a href="https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/9783d61f1b9c81231581fee026c8e8cb9499d265/bip-0085.mediawiki" rel="noopener nofollow">BIP85</a>
      deterministically derives application entropy from a BIP32 master root.
      COLDCARD's implementation in
      <a href="https://github.com/Coldcard/firmware/blob/9a88e1a5c32c69ba7ace7f22c26da90f8442b745/shared/drv_entro.py" rel="noopener nofollow"><code>shared/drv_entro.py</code></a>
      derives the selected BIP85 path from the active wallet secret and then
      applies the specified HMAC-SHA512 transform. It does not generate an
      independent random root for the child wallet.<Claim
        basis="verified"
        scope="COLDCARD's deterministic BIP85 derivation from the active BIP32 root"
        source="BIP85 and coldcard-firmware shared/drv_entro.py, both linked above"
        href="https://github.com/Coldcard/firmware/blob/9a88e1a5c32c69ba7ace7f22c26da90f8442b745/shared/drv_entro.py"
      />
    </p>

    <p>
      The consequence is conditional. If an affected active root can be recovered,
      its BIP85 children can be reproduced when their application and index paths
      are known or searched. If the active wallet included an undisclosed BIP39
      passphrase, that passphrase remains an independent input to the root. A
      migration inventory should therefore include BIP85-derived wallets rather
      than treating their different mnemonic words as independent provenance.<Claim
        basis="derived"
        scope="The conditional inheritance of recoverability and the resulting migration inventory"
        source="Deterministic BIP85 derivation applied to a recoverable active root; passphrase and path assumptions stated above"
      />
    </p>

    <p>
      <a href="/record/sources/benma-bip85-warning/">Benma</a> and
      <a href="/record/sources/kloaec-bip85-warning/">Kevin Loaec</a> each
      published incident-specific warnings about BIP85-derived wallets.<Claim
        basis="reported"
        scope="The incident-specific warnings to migrate BIP85-derived wallets"
        source="Benma and Kevin Loaec, separately captured primary X posts linked above"
        href="/record/sources/benma-bip85-warning/"
      />
    </p>
  </Disclosure>

  <p>
    The commit history behind the affected call surface, including an independent
    walkthrough by Dustin Dettmer, is on
    <a href="/response/developers/">the developer-response page</a>; the wider set
    of published analyses, with the commercial interests attached to each, is on
    <a href="/response/">the response page</a>.
  </p>
</Article>
